<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert Camus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1913">1913</a>–<a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert Camus (1913–1960) was a French writer and philosopher born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> to a poor settler family. His novels The Stranger and The Plague, both set in Algeria, and his essay The Myth of Sisyphus made him a leading figure of post-war literature, and he won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1957. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algerian-war">Algerian War</a> he called for a civilian truce and opposed both FLN terrorism and French repression.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-camus.jpg" alt="Albert Camus" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photograph by United Press International · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albert_Camus,_gagnant_de_prix_Nobel,_portrait_en_buste,_pos%C3%A9_au_bureau,_faisant_face_%C3%A0_gauche,_cigarette_de_tabagisme.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
